title: Tag syntax in simplified queries
tags: [[Simplified query language]]
kind: reference
summary: #X finds a tag by fuzzy path, #=P a tag and its subtree by full path, ##=P exactly one tag, #!X the records that deny a tag.

The default grammar has three tag forms. All select the records whose `tag` ref points
at a tag entry (`mf_schema = "tag"`) chosen by its `path` ([[Hierarchical tags]]):

|!Form |!Selects |!Expands to |
|`#jazz` |a tag found ''fuzzily'' along its path — name a leaf without its ancestors |`tag -> (mf_schema = "tag" AND osm(path, "jazz"))` |
|`#=music/jazz` |that tag ''and every tag below it'', by full path from the root |`… path =>* "music/jazz"` |
|`##=music/jazz` |''exactly'' that tag |`… path = "music/jazz"` |
|`#!jazz` |the records that ''deny'' a tag found fuzzily, like `#jazz` (a `negative_tag` ref) |`negative_tag -> (mf_schema = "tag" AND osm(path, "jazz"))` |

In `#X`, a `/` separates ordered-substring terms rather than anchoring a path:
`#mu/ja` matches a path holding `mu` then `ja`, in order, not necessarily contiguous nor
from the root ([[Ordered substring matching]]). A quoted value is one contiguous term:
`#"jeux video"`. A word may hold any letter, accented or not, so `#écriture` needs no
quotes ([[Simplified grammar notation]]). `#=` and `##=` take an unquoted slash-path. The three sigils are
distinct tokens, so their order in the grammar does not matter.

To negate, keep a space — `! #jazz`: `!#` lexes as one symbol.

`! #jazz` and `#!jazz` are not the same question. `! #jazz` holds every record without
the tag, including the ones never asked about it; `#!jazz` only those that answered
''no'' — `mf tag deny`, or the tagging scripts ([[Hierarchical tags]]). The records not
yet asked are `! #jazz AND ! #!jazz`. Like `#jazz`, `#!jazz` looks at the tag named and
not its neighbours: denying `music` also says "not jazz"
([[Tag subsumption and exclusivity]]), but a record denying only `music` is found by `#!music`, not `#!jazz`.

`#=` exists because tagging a record with a narrower tag drops the broader one, so
"music/jazz or anything under it" is the question usually meant. Before August 2026
bare `#X` was the subtree form and `##X` the exact one; the fuzzy `#X` replaced them,
and `path` stopped being the file path's alias (it is `p` now) so that it could name
the tag hierarchy's field.
